I like the idea, though I had a couple of comments about the
implementation:

L wrote:
> Currently, the criteria for innumeracy is "Int < 8". I have some
> reservations about this, but I can't think of anything more
> appropriate.

IIRC, the original idea was that Cavemen should be the ones who are
"innumerate."  I tend to think that that's a good idea, and it would
be an interesting defining point of the class.  Maybe being innumerate
should be limited to cavemen of any intelligence (no matter how smart
he is, he never learned to count), or really dumb specimens of other
classes (probably lower than 7 -- say 5 or below.)

> * You currently cannot wish for "some", "several", "many", "lots of",
> "hundreds of" and "thousands of" a certain item. Maybe a little later.

In fiction, one of the common themes when dealing with characters
who can't count is that they can't distinguish between large
numbers.  For instance, the farmer who saw the enemy army can't say
if there were two hundred or twenty thousand soldiers.  In keeping with
that, I suggest that you drop "hundreds" and "thousands" completely,
and just let all of them be "lots."

Not, of course, like it is that big a deal, since there are not that
many cases where numbers that big come up.  Gold pieces, and some
characters end up with that many missile weapons.  Disregarding
extinctionists and farmers, I can't think of any other places where
numbers >200 regularly come up.

One other thought -- when paying for goods, should shopkeepers
somtimes rip off the innumerate by taking more money than was agreed 
to?

